{
  "title": "Python deps: uv vs Poetry vs pip-tools",
  "content": "Inherited serverless cold setups tend to come with no explanation, just a working system and a long reverse-engineering session. Production gives you neither a clean environment nor a patient timeline. Here's the root cause, not just the symptom.\n\n## The confusion: three tools, three mental models\n\nAsk five Python developers how they manage dependencies and you'll get five different answers. One swears by `requirements.txt` and `pip-compile`. Another has a `pyproject.toml` with `[tool.poetry]` and a `poetry.lock`. A third has started using `uv` and now runs `uv sync` instead of `pip install`. All three work. None of them interoperate cleanly. The confusion isn't about which tool is \"best\" in the abstract — it's that each tool encodes a different model of what a dependency *is*, and teams mix them without realizing the models conflict.\n\nThis post resolves that confusion by laying out the underlying model each tool uses, showing where the models diverge in practice, and giving a decision framework for picking one per project. The real problem it solves: you cannot safely mix `pip`, `poetry`, and `uv` in the same repository without knowing which one owns the lockfile and which one owns the virtualenv.\n\n## The underlying model: resolver, lockfile, environment\n\nEvery Python dependency tool does three things, and the differences live in how it does them:\n\n1. **Resolve** a set of version constraints into concrete pins.\n2. **Record** those pins in a lockfile (or not).\n3. **Install** the pins into an environment (a virtualenv, usually).\n\n`pip` historically only did step 3 well, with a resolver bolted on in recent versions. `pip-tools` adds step 1 and 2 via `pip-compile`, producing a `requirements.txt` with hashes. `Poetry` and `uv` do all three, but they disagree on file formats and on whether the lockfile is authoritative.\n\nThe key mental model: **the lockfile is the contract**. If two tools disagree about which file is the lockfile, you have two contracts, and your CI will eventually install a different set of packages than your laptop. That is the failure mode behind most \"works on my machine\" incidents in Python projects.\n\nA second axis is **where the virtualenv lives**. `pip` and `pip-tools` install into whatever environment is active (`$VIRTUAL_ENV`). `Poetry` creates and manages a virtualenv in a cache directory by default, unless `virtualenvs.in-project` is set. `uv` defaults to a `.venv` in the project root. If you run `poetry install` and then `uv sync` in the same repo, you may end up with two virtualenvs and a confusing `python -c \"import sys; print(sys.prefix)\"` result.\n\n## Where the models actually diverge\n\n### pip-tools: constraints in, pins out\n\n`pip-tools` is the smallest conceptual leap from plain `pip`. You write abstract requirements in `requirements.in`:\n\n```text\n# requirements.in\nrequests>=2.31\nflask>=3.0,<4\n```\n\nThen compile them:\n\n```bash\npip-compile requirements.in --generate-hashes -o requirements.txt\n```\n\nThe output `requirements.txt` contains exact pins with hashes, including transitive dependencies. You install with `pip install -r requirements.txt`. There is no separate lockfile concept — the compiled `requirements.txt` *is* the lockfile.\n\nThis model is honest and transparent. The downside is that `pip-tools` does not manage your virtualenv, does not know about `pyproject.toml` dependency groups, and does not handle editable installs of your own package gracefully. It also does not resolve platform-specific markers into a single lockfile the way Poetry and uv do; you may need separate compiled files per Python version or platform if your dependency tree is conditional.\n\n### Poetry: project metadata plus lockfile\n\nPoetry treats `pyproject.toml` as the source of truth for both your package metadata and your dependency constraints. `poetry.lock` records the resolved graph. `poetry install` reads the lockfile and installs into a Poetry-managed virtualenv.\n\nThe lockfile format is Poetry-specific. It is not a `requirements.txt`, and `pip install -r` cannot consume it directly. Poetry can export one (`poetry export -f requirements.txt`), but the export is a one-way projection: the lockfile remains authoritative, and the exported file can drift if you forget to re-export.\n\nPoetry's resolver is stricter than pip's about conflicting constraints, which is good for catching problems early and annoying when a transitive dependency has a loose upper bound. The common complaint — resolution taking a long time on large graphs — is a real tradeoff of doing full backtracking resolution rather than pip's more permissive approach.\n\n### uv: resolver plus environment manager plus installer\n\n`uv` is a single binary that resolves, locks, and installs. It reads `pyproject.toml` for project metadata and dependencies, writes `uv.lock`, and creates a `.venv` in the project root by default. `uv sync` brings the environment in line with the lockfile; `uv lock` re-resolves; `uv add` and `uv remove` edit `pyproject.toml` and update the lock together.\n\nThe important difference from Poetry is that `uv` is also a drop-in replacement for `pip` and `pip-tools` in many workflows: `uv pip install`, `uv pip compile`, and `uv pip sync` operate on `requirements.txt` files. That means you can adopt it incrementally — start by replacing `pip install` with `uv pip install` for speed, then move to the project/lockfile model when ready.\n\nThe `uv.lock` format is also not a `requirements.txt`. Like Poetry, you can export if you need to interoperate with tools that expect one.\n\n### Side-by-side\n\n| Concern | pip-tools | Poetry | uv |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Source of constraints | `requirements.in` | `pyproject.toml` | `pyproject.toml` |\n| Lockfile | compiled `requirements.txt` | `poetry.lock` | `uv.lock` |\n| Manages virtualenv | No | Yes (cache dir by default) | Yes (`.venv` by default) |\n| Consumable by `pip install -r` | Yes (it is one) | Via `poetry export` | Via `uv export` |\n| Handles editable self-install | Awkward | Yes | Yes |\n| Drop-in `pip` replacement | No | No | Yes (`uv pip`) |\n\n## Worked example: the same project in all three\n\nAssume a small Flask service with two direct dependencies and one dev-only dependency. The `pyproject.toml` for the Poetry and uv cases is similar:\n\n```toml\n[project]\nname = \"example-service\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.11\"\ndependencies = [\n    \"flask>=3.0,<4\",\n    \"requests>=2.31\",\n]\n\n[dependency-groups]\ndev = [\n    \"pytest>=8\",\n]\n```\n\n**pip-tools workflow:**\n\n```text\n# requirements.in\nflask>=3.0,<4\nrequests>=2.31\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements-dev.in\n-c requirements.in\npytest>=8\n```\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npip install pip-tools\npip-compile requirements.in --generate-hashes -o requirements.txt\npip-compile requirements-dev.in --generate-hashes -o requirements-dev.txt\npip install -r requirements-dev.txt\n```\n\nNote the `-c requirements.in` line: it constrains the dev file to the same versions as the runtime file, which is the pip-tools idiom for keeping dev and runtime pins aligned. Without it, `pip-compile` may resolve `pytest` against a different `flask` version than `requirements.txt` has.\n\n**Poetry workflow:**\n\n```bash\npoetry add \"flask>=3.0,<4\" \"requests>=2.31\"\npoetry add --group dev \"pytest>=8\"\npoetry install\n```\n\nPoetry writes the constraints into `pyproject.toml`, resolves, and writes `poetry.lock`. On CI, `poetry install --sync` makes the environment match the lockfile exactly, removing packages not in it. Without `--sync`, Poetry will leave extra packages in place, which is a subtle source of drift.\n\n**uv workflow:**\n\n```bash\nuv init --python 3.11\nuv add \"flask>=3.0,<4\" \"requests>=2.31\"\nuv add --dev \"pytest>=8\"\nuv sync\n```\n\n`uv sync` creates `.venv` if it doesn't exist and installs exactly the locked set. `uv run pytest` executes a command inside the project environment without needing to activate it, which is the cleanest CI pattern of the three.\n\n## Common misreadings\n\n**\"The lockfile pins everything, so my environment is reproducible.\"** Only if every install path reads the lockfile. A common failure: CI runs `pip install -e .` (which resolves fresh, ignoring the lockfile) instead of `uv sync` or `poetry install`. The fix is to make the lockfile-reading command the only install step in CI and to fail the build if `pyproject.toml` and the lockfile are out of sync. `uv lock --check` and `poetry check --lock` exist for this; run them before install.\n\n**\"I can run `poetry install` and `uv sync` in the same repo.\"** You can, but they will disagree about which virtualenv to use and which lockfile is authoritative. Pick one tool per repository and enforce it with a pre-commit hook or a CI check that fails if the other tool's lockfile appears. A repo with both `poetry.lock` and `uv.lock` is a repo where nobody knows which one is real.\n\n**\"pip-tools doesn't need a virtualenv manager because I activate one manually.\"** True, but the activation step is exactly what breaks in CI and in editor integrations. If your editor runs `python` from the wrong interpreter, `pip-compile` will still work (it doesn't need the environment) but `pip install -r` will install into the wrong place. The `pip-tools` model pushes environment management onto you; that's a feature for some teams and a footgun for others.\n\n**\"uv is just a faster pip.\"** It is a faster pip *and* a project manager. If you only use `uv pip install`, you get the speed and none of the lockfile benefits. The adoption path that gets the most value is: replace `pip install` with `uv pip install` first (zero risk, immediate speedup), then migrate to `uv sync` with `uv.lock` once the team is comfortable.\n\n**\"Poetry's resolver is slow, so I should switch.\"** Resolution time depends on graph size and constraint tightness. If a project resolves slowly, the usual cause is a transitive dependency with a wide version range that forces backtracking. Loosening your own constraints rarely helps; tightening the problematic transitive constraint via a direct pin often does. Switching tools fixes the symptom for one project and reproduces it on the next large graph.\n\n## A decision framework\n\nUse this to pick one tool per project. The first matching row wins.\n\n1. **You need a single binary that also replaces `pip` and works on Windows CI without a Python toolchain preinstalled.** Use `uv`. The `uv pip` compatibility layer means you can migrate incrementally, and `uv run` removes the activation step from CI.\n2. **You publish a library to PyPI and need PEP 621 metadata plus a lockfile for development.** Use `Poetry` or `uv` — both read `pyproject.toml` `[project]` metadata. If your team already has Poetry lockfiles and CI, stay on Poetry; migration cost is real and the benefit is mostly speed.\n3. **You have an existing `requirements.txt`-based pipeline (Docker images, Lambda layers, legacy CI) and cannot change the install step.** Use `pip-tools`. It produces a `requirements.txt` that every downstream tool already understands, with hashes for `pip install --require-hashes`.\n4. **You have a monorepo with multiple packages and shared constraints.** Use `uv` workspaces or Poetry's path dependencies, not `pip-tools`. `pip-tools` has no concept of a workspace and you will end up compiling per-package files and manually keeping them aligned.\n5. **You are on an air-gapped or heavily restricted network.** `pip-tools` with a local wheel mirror is the simplest model because the install step is plain `pip`. `uv` and Poetry both support custom indexes, but verify the resolver behavior against your mirror before committing.\n\nWhat to avoid: mixing tools in one repo, keeping both a `requirements.txt` and a lockfile as \"authoritative\" without a sync check, and letting CI resolve fresh instead of installing from the lockfile.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Can I use uv with an existing requirements.txt?**\n\nYes. `uv pip compile requirements.in -o requirements.txt` produces the same kind of hashed output as `pip-compile`, and `uv pip sync requirements.txt` installs exactly that set. This is the lowest-risk adoption path: keep your existing files, swap the commands, and measure the speed difference before deciding whether to move to `uv.lock` and `pyproject.toml`.\n\n**Why does poetry install leave packages that aren't in the lockfile?**\n\nBy default, `poetry install` adds missing packages but does not remove extras. `poetry install --sync` removes anything not in the lockfile, making the environment match exactly. CI should use `--sync`; local development often doesn't, which is why environments drift over time.\n\n**How do I stop pip from resolving fresh in CI?**\n\nReplace `pip install -e .` or `pip install .` with an install from the lockfile: `uv sync --frozen`, `poetry install --sync`, or `pip install -r requirements.txt --require-hashes`. The `--frozen` flag on `uv sync` fails if `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` are out of sync, which catches the case where someone edited dependencies without re-locking.\n\n**What is the difference between a lockfile and a constraints file?**\n\nA lockfile records the full resolved graph, including transitive dependencies, at exact versions (and usually hashes). A constraints file (`-c` in pip, or `[tool.poetry.dependencies]` entries) only narrows what the resolver is allowed to pick; it does not pin the full graph. `pip-tools` uses constraints as input and produces a lockfile-shaped `requirements.txt` as output. Conflating the two is the most common source of \"why did CI install a different version\" confusion.\n\n## The next 30 minutes\n\nOpen the repository you deploy most often and check whether it has more than one of `requirements.txt`, `poetry.lock`, or `uv.lock` at the root. If it does, pick the one your CI actually installs from and delete or archive the others in a branch — then add a CI step that fails if the wrong lockfile reappears. That single check prevents the most common class of Python dependency drift before it reaches production.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Docker documentation](https://docs.docker.com/)\n- [AWS Lambda Developer Guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
